OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026", "title": "Symmetry of semialgebraic bounded domains with compact quotient", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Symmetry-of-semialgebraic-bounded-domains-with-compact-quotient-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "058", "family_title": "Semialgebraic universal covers and bounded domains", "abstract_html": "Every nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset of a complex affine variety admitting a properly discontinuous cocompact group of biholomorphisms is smooth and biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain. This answers the bounded-domain question of Kollár and Pardon affirmatively.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(U\\) is smooth and is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A complex affine variety means a reduced complex algebraic set, equipped with a fixed closed algebraic embedding into \\(\\mathbb C^N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Semialgebraicity is understood in the real and imaginary coordinates of this embedding, and boundedness is understood in the same embedding.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be a nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset of a complex affine variety, where openness is in the complex-space topology.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Gamma\\) be a group of biholomorphisms of \\(U\\). Assume that its action is proper when \\(\\Gamma\\) is given the discrete topology and that \\(U/\\Gamma\\) is compact. Finite stabilizers are allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">A bounded symmetric domain is a bounded domain in a complex vector space admitting, at every point, a holomorphic involution for which that point is an isolated fixed point. A point is allowed as the zero-dimensional bounded symmetric domain.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(U\\) is smooth and is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(U\\) is zero-dimensional, connectedness makes it a point, which is the zero-dimensional bounded symmetric domain.</p><p>Otherwise, Proposition 1 proves that \\(U\\) is smooth, lies in the smooth locus of a single irreducible affine component, and has a compact covering set for the given action.</p><p>Proposition 7 identifies \\(U\\) biholomorphically with\n\\[\nD=\n\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k:\n\\operatorname{Im}w-H(z,z)\\in C\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(C\\) is a nonempty connected open cone invariant under positive dilations, and where independent real linear functionals satisfy\n\\[\n\\ell_j(y)&gt;0\\quad(y\\in C),\n\\qquad\n\\ell_j(H(z,z))\\geq c\\|z\\|^2\n\\]\nfor a common \\(c&gt;0\\).</p><p>Conjugate the given action on \\(U\\) by this biholomorphism. Proper discontinuity and the existence of a compact covering set are preserved by conjugacy. Thus \\(D\\) is divisible, including when point stabilizers are nontrivial. No replacement by a free action and no assumption that the quotient is a manifold are needed.</p><p>Proposition 8 applies and proves that \\(D\\), and hence \\(U\\), is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a compact set \\(K\\subset U\\) such that \\(\\Gamma K=U\\).", "statement": "<p>**Smoothness; original Proposition 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) be a nonempty connected bounded open subset of a reduced complex affine algebraic set \\(A\\subset\\mathbb C^N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that a group \\(\\Gamma\\) of biholomorphisms acts properly discontinuously on \\(U\\) and that \\(U/\\Gamma\\) is compact.</p><p class=\"li\">Semialgebraicity is not required for this proposition.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(U\\) is not a point.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a compact set \\(K\\subset U\\) such that \\(\\Gamma K=U\\). Moreover, \\(U\\) is a complex manifold of positive dimension and lies in the smooth locus of a single irreducible component \\(V\\) of \\(A\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The quotient map is open, and the images of relatively compact open neighborhoods in \\(U\\) cover \\(U/\\Gamma\\). Finitely many suffice. Their closures give a compact set \\(K\\subset U\\) with \\(\\Gamma K=U\\). This does not require the action to be free.</p><p>Choose a positive-dimensional irreducible component \\(V_0\\) whose smooth points not shared with another component meet \\(U\\). Such a component exists: these points are dense in each positive-dimensional component, whereas a zero-dimensional irreducible component is isolated and cannot meet the connected nonsingleton \\(U\\).</p><p>There is a polynomial \\(P\\) vanishing on the other components and on the singular locus of \\(V_0\\), but not identically on \\(V_0\\). In particular, \\(P\\) is nonzero somewhere in \\(U\\).</p><p>Maximize\n\\[\n\\log|P(q)|+\\|q\\|^2\n\\]\non the compact set \\(\\overline U\\), with value \\(-\\infty\\) at zeros of \\(P\\). A maximizer \\(p\\) has \\(P(p)\\neq0\\), so it is a smooth, unshared point of \\(V_0\\). It belongs to \\(\\partial U\\): near such a point the function is strictly plurisubharmonic, and hence cannot have an interior maximum.</p><p>The entire function\n\\[\nh(q)=\\frac{P(q)}{P(p)}\n\\exp\\bigl(2\\overline p\\cdot(q-p)\\bigr)\n\\]\nsatisfies\n\\[\n|h(q)|\\leq\\exp(-\\|q-p\\|^2)\n\\quad(q\\in\\overline U),\\qquad h(p)=1.\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nIndeed, the logarithm of the left-hand side is at most\n\\[\n\\|p\\|^2-\\|q\\|^2\n+2\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(\\overline p\\cdot(q-p)\\bigr).\n\\]</p><p>Choose \\(q_j\\in U\\) tending to \\(p\\), and write\n\\[\nq_j=\\gamma_j k_j,\\qquad \\gamma_j\\in\\Gamma,\\quad k_j\\in K.\n\\]\nAfter extraction, \\(k_j\\to k\\in K\\), and Lemma 1.1 gives locally uniform convergence of \\(h\\circ\\gamma_j\\). Equicontinuity at the moving points \\(k_j\\) shows that the limit has value \\(1\\) at \\(k\\). It is therefore identically \\(1\\) on \\(U\\). The peak inequality (2.1) implies that \\(\\gamma_j\\) converges to \\(p\\) uniformly on every compact subset of \\(U\\).</p><p>For each \\(q\\in U\\), the point \\(\\gamma_j(q)\\) eventually lies in a smooth ambient neighborhood of \\(p\\). Since a biholomorphism preserves smoothness of the complex space, \\(q\\) is smooth. A smooth point belongs to exactly one irreducible component of the ambient variety; the component is locally constant on \\(U\\), and hence constant by connectedness. This gives \\(V\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "This family is locally equicontinuous.", "statement": "<p>**Bounded holomorphic functions on a reduced affine space; original Lemma 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, replace the domain and group-action setting of Proposition 1 by an arbitrary open subset \\(W\\) of a reduced complex affine algebraic set.</p><p class=\"li\">Consider holomorphic functions \\(h\\) on \\(W\\) satisfying \\(|h|\\leq 1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>This family is locally equicontinuous. Every sequence in the family has a subsequence converging uniformly on compact subsets to a continuous function \\(h_\\infty\\).</p><p>If \\(h_\\infty(q)=1\\) at a point \\(q\\in W\\), then \\(h_\\infty=1\\) in a neighborhood of \\(q\\). In particular, if \\(W\\) is connected, then \\(h_\\infty\\equiv1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>We give the argument at a possibly singular and nonnormal point \\(q\\). Work first on one irreducible algebraic component \\(V_0\\) through \\(q\\), of positive dimension \\(d\\). Noether normalization [Stacks, Lemma 10.115.4, Tag 00OY] gives a finite algebraic map\n\\[\n\\pi:V_0\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C^d.\n\\]\nTranslate so that \\(\\pi(q)=0\\). By properness and finiteness, we may restrict over a sufficiently small polydisc \\(B\\) about \\(0\\) and retain just the part \\(\\Omega\\) near \\(q\\), with\n\\[\n\\pi:\\Omega\\longrightarrow B\n\\quad\\text{proper and finite},\\qquad\n\\pi^{-1}(0)\\cap\\Omega=\\{q\\},\\qquad\n\\Omega\\subset W.\n\\]\nTo make this restriction, take disjoint neighborhoods of the finitely many points over \\(0\\) and shrink \\(B\\) until its entire inverse image lies in their union. The part near \\(q\\) is then both open and closed in that inverse image.</p><p>Outside a proper algebraic hypersurface \\(E\\) in \\(B\\), the restricted map is a covering with a fixed number \\(r\\geq1\\) of holomorphic sheets. This follows also directly from separability of the finite function-field extension: discard the denominators and discriminant of a primitive element.</p><p>For \\(|h|\\leq1\\), form the monic polynomial of its sheet values:\n\\[\nP_h(z,T)\n=\n\\prod_{x\\in\\pi^{-1}(z)\\cap\\Omega}(T-h(x))\n=\nT^r+\\sum_{\\nu=0}^{r-1}a_{\\nu,h}(z)T^\\nu,\n\\qquad z\\in B\\setminus E.\n\\]\nThe coefficients are single-valued holomorphic functions bounded by constants depending only on \\(r\\). They extend holomorphically across \\(E\\) by the removable-singularity theorem for bounded holomorphic functions. One elementary proof uses a complex line through the point that is not contained in \\(E\\), a small circle on that line missing \\(E\\), and the Cauchy integral on nearby parallel circles.</p><p>Properness and continuity of \\(h\\) give\n\\[\nP_h(0,T)=(T-h(q))^r.\n\\]\nThe identity \\(P_h(\\pi(x),h(x))=0\\) holds on all of \\(\\Omega\\) by continuity, since the sheet locus is dense. Cauchy estimates for the uniformly bounded coefficients, on a smaller polydisc, therefore yield\n\\[\n|h(x)-h(q)|^r\n=\n|P_h(0,h(x))-P_h(\\pi(x),h(x))|\n\\leq C\\|\\pi(x)\\|.\n\\]\nHere \\(C\\) is independent of \\(h\\). This proves equicontinuity at \\(q\\) on \\(V_0\\). There are only finitely many components through \\(q\\); applying the argument to each proves the assertion on their union. A zero-dimensional component requires no argument. Local compactness, a countable compact exhaustion, and the Arzelà–Ascoli theorem now give compact convergence of a subsequence.</p><p>For the maximum assertion, use the same chart and consider the sheet average\n\\[\nb_h(z)=\\frac1r\\sum_{x\\in\\pi^{-1}(z)\\cap\\Omega}h(x).\n\\]\nIt extends holomorphically to \\(B\\), satisfies \\(|b_h|\\leq1\\), and has \\(b_h(0)=h(q)\\). If \\(h_j\\to h_\\infty\\) locally uniformly and \\(h_\\infty(q)=1\\), ordinary normal-family compactness on \\(B\\) gives, after another extraction, a holomorphic limit of the sheet averages with value \\(1\\) at \\(0\\). The usual maximum principle makes that limit identically \\(1\\).</p><p>On every regular fiber, an average of numbers in the closed unit disc can equal \\(1\\) only if every number equals \\(1\\). Thus \\(h_\\infty=1\\) on the sheet locus near \\(q\\), hence everywhere near \\(q\\) by continuity. Apply this on every component through \\(q\\). Finally, \\(\\{h_\\infty=1\\}\\) is both closed and open in \\(W\\), which proves the connected case. No identification of weakly holomorphic functions with holomorphic functions on a nonnormal space was used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(d_U\\) is a finite distance inducing the usual topology of \\(U\\), and every closed \\(d_U\\)-ball is compact.", "statement": "<p>**A proper intrinsic distance and disc continuity; original Proposition 2.3 and Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^N\\) be an irreducible complex affine variety, and let \\(U\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be a nonempty connected bounded open subset of positive complex dimension.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose that a group \\(\\Gamma\\) of biholomorphisms has a compact covering set \\(K\\subset U\\), meaning \\(\\Gamma K=U\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Semialgebraicity is not required.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathbb D\\) be the unit disc, and let \\(\\rho\\) be its Poincaré distance.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(x,y\\in U\\), define \\(d_U(x,y)\\) as the infimum of\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{s}\\rho(\\alpha_j,\\beta_j)\n\\]\nover finite chains of holomorphic maps \\(a_j:\\mathbb D\\to U\\) satisfying\n\\[\na_1(\\alpha_1)=x,\\qquad\na_s(\\beta_s)=y,\\qquad\na_j(\\beta_j)=a_{j+1}(\\alpha_{j+1}).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(d_U\\) is a finite distance inducing the usual topology of \\(U\\), and every closed \\(d_U\\)-ball is compact. Holomorphic maps decrease this chain-of-discs distance, and biholomorphisms preserve it.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. The following continuity principle holds. Let \\(\\Omega\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be a smooth coordinate neighborhood, and let\n\\[\nA:[0,1]\\times\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\longrightarrow\\Omega\n\\]\nbe continuous, with each \\(A_t=A(t,\\cdot)\\) holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\). If\n\\[\nA_t(\\partial\\mathbb D)\\subset U\\quad(0\\leq t\\leq1),\n\\qquad\nA_0(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset U,\n\\]\nthen \\(A_t(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset U\\) for every \\(t\\). In particular, holomorphic extension through the boundary circle is not required for continuous deformations of closed \\(C^1\\) analytic discs.</p>", "proof": "<p>Coordinate balls give finite disc chains locally, hence globally by connectedness. Contraction under holomorphic maps is built into the definition, and applying contraction to a biholomorphism and its inverse gives invariance.</p><p>Bounded ambient coordinate functions and the Schwarz lemma give a constant \\(c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\nd_U(x,y)\\geq c\\|x-y\\|.\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\nFor example, send each bounded coordinate to \\(\\mathbb D\\); the Poincaré distance there dominates a constant times Euclidean distance, and sum along a chain. Conversely, small affine complex discs in a manifold chart give a local upper bound tending to zero with the coordinate separation. Thus \\(d_U\\) is a distance and induces the original topology.</p><p>Choose a relatively compact neighborhood of \\(K\\) in \\(U\\). Inequality (2.2) and compactness of \\(K\\) give an \\(r&gt;0\\) for which every closed \\(r\\)-ball centered in \\(K\\) lies in that neighborhood’s compact closure. Such a ball is closed in the original topology, hence compact. Translating by \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that every closed \\(r\\)-ball in \\(U\\) is compact, with the same \\(r\\).</p><p>We spell out why this uniform local statement implies properness. Fix \\(x\\in U\\), and suppose that \\(\\overline B_{d_U}(x,R)\\) is compact. Cover it by finitely many open \\(r/4\\)-balls centered at \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_l\\).</p><p>For \\(d_U(x,y)\\leq R+r/4\\), choose a disc chain of total Poincaré length less than \\(d_U(x,y)+r/4\\). Replace each disc link by a Poincaré geodesic segment. If the total length exceeds \\(R\\), the point at accumulated length \\(R\\) lies in \\(\\overline B_{d_U}(x,R)\\), and the remaining length is less than \\(r/2\\). Hence \\(y\\) belongs to an \\(r\\)-ball centered at some \\(x_i\\). If the total length is at most \\(R\\), the same conclusion is immediate. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\overline B_{d_U}(x,R+r/4)\n\\subset\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{l}\\overline B_{d_U}(x_i,r).\n\\]\nThe set on the left is closed and the union on the right is compact. Starting at \\(R=r\\) and iterating proves compactness for every radius.</p><p>The hypotheses on \\(d_U\\) in Lemma 2.1 have now been proved. Applying that lemma gives the stated continuity principle.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(A_t(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset U\\) for every \\(t\\in[0,1]\\).", "statement": "<p>**Continuity principle under properness; original Lemma 2.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the smooth bounded-domain setting and the chain-of-discs distance \\(d_U\\) of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, assume additionally that \\(d_U\\) is a finite proper distance inducing the usual topology of \\(U\\). This is an assumption of the lemma, not a conclusion used without proof.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be a smooth coordinate neighborhood.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nA:[0,1]\\times\\overline{\\mathbb D}\\longrightarrow\\Omega\n\\]\nbe continuous, with each \\(A_t\\) holomorphic on \\(\\mathbb D\\), and suppose\n\\[\nA_t(\\partial\\mathbb D)\\subset U\\quad(0\\leq t\\leq1),\n\\qquad A_0(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset U.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(A_t(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset U\\) for every \\(t\\in[0,1]\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The set\n\\[\nT=\\{t:A_t(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset U\\}\n\\]\nis relatively open in \\([0,1]\\) by compactness of the closed disc.</p><p>To see that it is closed, let \\(t_j\\in T\\) tend to \\(t_*\\). The boundary condition and continuity give a fixed collar\n\\[\n1-\\varepsilon\\leq|\\zeta|\\leq1\n\\]\nwhose images, for \\(t\\) near \\(t_*\\), lie in a compact set \\(L\\subset U\\). Fix \\(\\zeta_0\\) in the interior of this collar and \\(x_0\\in U\\). The number\n\\[\nM=\\max_{x\\in L}d_U(x_0,x)\n\\]\nis finite. For each fixed \\(\\zeta\\in\\mathbb D\\), the earlier discs take values in \\(U\\), so Schwarz contraction gives\n\\[\nd_U(x_0,A_{t_j}(\\zeta))\n\\leq M+\\rho(\\zeta_0,\\zeta).\n\\]\nProperness places these values in a fixed compact subset of \\(U\\). Their ambient limit \\(A_{t_*}(\\zeta)\\) therefore belongs to \\(U\\). The boundary already lies in \\(U\\), so \\(t_*\\in T\\).</p><p>Since \\(T\\) is nonempty, open, and closed in \\([0,1]\\), it is all of \\([0,1]\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Every boundary cell \\(S\\) has one of the following properties:", "statement": "<p>**Semialgebraic boundary preparation; original Proposition 3.3, with the boundary-cell alternative of original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^N\\) be irreducible of complex dimension \\(n\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be a nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset.</p><p class=\"li\">Decompose \\(\\partial U\\cap V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) into finitely many connected real-analytic semialgebraic cells with algebraic analytic parametrizations.</p><p class=\"li\">An intrinsic null set in a cell means a set of measure zero in its real-analytic coordinate charts.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every boundary cell \\(S\\) has one of the following properties:\n\\[\nT_pS+iT_pS=T_pV\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\noutside an intrinsic null set of \\(S\\), or \\(S\\) is contained in a proper complex algebraic subset of \\(V\\).</p><p>For a cell in the first alternative, at a point satisfying (3.1), put\n\\[\nk=2n-\\dim_{\\mathbb R}S,\\qquad m=n-k,\\qquad 1\\leq k\\leq n.\n\\]\nThere are local semialgebraic holomorphic coordinates\n\\[\n(z_0,w_0)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k\n\\]\nin which a patch of \\(S\\) is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}w_0=\\varphi(s),\n\\qquad s=(z_0,\\operatorname{Re}w_0)\\in B,\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(B\\) is an open real parameter box and \\(\\varphi\\) is real analytic and semialgebraic. The patch can be chosen so that its intersection with every fixed-\\(z_0\\) slice is totally real in \\(\\mathbb C^k\\).</p><p>Choose \\(\\rho&gt;0\\) so that all points below with \\(s\\in B\\) and \\(|v|&lt;\\rho\\) remain in the chart. Define\n\\[\nv=\\operatorname{Im}w_0-\\varphi(s),\\qquad\ns=(z_0,x_0),\\quad x_0=\\operatorname{Re}w_0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nA_s=\n\\left\\{\nv\\in\\mathbb R^k:\n|v|&lt;\\rho,\\ \n(z_0,x_0+i(\\varphi(s)+v))\\in U\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nThese are open semialgebraic fibers and \\(0\\notin A_s\\).</p><p>For almost every \\(s_*\\in B\\), both of the following hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. There are a neighborhood \\(B_*\\subset B\\) of \\(s_*\\), a number \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and a real-analytic map\n\\[\n\\psi:B_*\\times(-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R^k\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\psi(s,0)=0,\\qquad\n\\psi(s,u)\\in A_s\n\\quad(s\\in B_*,\\ 0&lt;u&lt;\\varepsilon).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. Set\n\\[\nE(s,t)=\\mathbb R^k\\setminus t^{-1}A_s,\\qquad\nd(s,t;v)=\\min\\{1,\\operatorname{dist}(v,E(s,t))\\}.\n\\]\nThere is a \\(1\\)-Lipschitz function \\(d_*:\\mathbb R^k\\to[0,1]\\) such that, for every compact \\(L\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{\\substack{s\\to s_*\\\\ t\\downarrow0}}\n\\sup_{v\\in L}|d(s,t;v)-d_*(v)|=0.\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nThe set\n\\[\nF=\\{v:d_*(v)=0\\}\n\\]\nis closed, contains \\(0\\), and is invariant under every positive real dilation.</p><p>In particular, if \\(s_j\\to s_*\\), \\(t_j\\downarrow0\\), and \\(v_j\\to v\\), then\n\\[\nd(s_j,t_j;v_j)\\longrightarrow d_*(v).\n\\]\nIf \\(v\\in F\\), then\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(v_j,E(s_j,t_j))\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nIf \\(v\\notin F\\), some fixed ball about \\(v\\) is contained in \\(t_j^{-1}A_{s_j}\\) for every sufficiently large \\(j\\).</p><p>Using countably many patches of the form (3.2), these conclusions define an intrinsic full-measure set of points on every cell in the first alternative. Call those points **good**.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the semialgebraic facts of finite real-analytic cell decomposition with algebraic analytic parametrizations, semialgebraic selection, and generic algebraic analyticity of semialgebraic functions [Cos05, Sections 1.1–1.2]. These facts apply equally to descriptions using real quantifiers. Lower-dimensional exceptional sets have Lebesgue measure zero.</p><p>Lemma 3.1 gives the asserted alternative for each boundary cell. Every boundary cell has positive real codimension, since the boundary of an open set has empty interior. On a cell satisfying (3.1), the real implicit function theorem gives the graph coordinates (3.2), after putting the tangent space in the form \\(\\{\\operatorname{Im}w_0=0\\}\\). The coordinates are obtained locally from a linear projection of \\(V\\), followed by a complex-linear change. Shrinking the patch ensures that its intersection with every fixed-\\(z_0\\) slice is totally real. Choose \\(\\rho\\) and define \\(A_s\\) as in (3.3).</p><p>For the analytic interior graphs, the function\n\\[\na(s)=\\min\\{1,\\operatorname{dist}(0,A_s)\\},\n\\]\nwith value \\(1\\) for an empty fiber, is semialgebraic and continuous on an open full-measure subset \\(B'\\subset B\\). It is zero there. In fact, if \\(a(s_0)&gt;0\\) at a continuity point, it is bounded below near \\(s_0\\); but the boundary point with parameters \\((s_0,0)\\) is a limit of points of \\(U\\), whose parameters \\((s_j,v_j)\\) satisfy\n\\[\ns_j\\to s_0,\\qquad v_j\\to0.\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction.</p><p>On \\(B'\\times(0,t_0)\\), semialgebraic selection therefore supplies\n\\[\nv(s,t)\\in A_s,\\qquad |v(s,t)|&lt;t.\n\\]\nApply Lemma 3.2 to its finitely many coordinates. At almost every \\(s_*\\), this gives a jointly analytic map\n\\[\n\\psi(s,u)=v(s,u^l).\n\\]\nThe bound \\(|\\psi(s,u)|&lt;u^l\\) proves \\(\\psi(s,0)=0\\) for every \\(s\\) in its neighborhood.</p><p>For the joint distance limits, apply Lemma 3.2 separately to \\(d(s,t;v)\\) for every \\(v\\in\\mathbb Q^k\\). Their exceptional sets have a null union. At a remaining \\(s_*\\), each rational test has a limit along every joint approach\n\\[\n(s,t)\\to(s_*,0),\\qquad t&gt;0.\n\\]\nFor every \\(s,t\\),\n\\[\n|d(s,t;v)-d(s,t;v')|\\leq|v-v'|.\n\\]\nThe rational limits therefore extend uniquely to a \\(1\\)-Lipschitz function \\(d_*\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^k\\). A finite rational net in a compact set, together with this inequality, proves (3.4). This argument uses only finitely many test neighborhoods at a time; it does not require a common neighborhood for all rational tests.</p><p>The zero set \\(F\\) is closed and contains \\(0\\), since \\(0\\in E(s,t)\\). For \\(c&gt;0\\), the exact identity\n\\[\nE(s,ct)=c^{-1}E(s,t)\n\\]\nshows, on taking \\(s=s_*\\) and \\(t\\downarrow0\\), that the distance from \\(v\\) to the left-hand side tends to zero if and only if the distance from \\(cv\\) to \\(E(s_*,t)\\) does. Truncation at \\(1\\) does not affect vanishing of a limit. Consequently,\n\\[\nv\\in F\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad cv\\in F.\n\\]</p><p>The assertion for moving vectors follows from the same Lipschitz inequality. If \\(d_*(v)=0\\), convergence of the truncated distance to zero is convergence of the untruncated distance to zero. If \\(d_*(v)&gt;0\\), the distance of \\(v\\) from \\(E(s_j,t_j)\\) is eventually at least \\(d_*(v)/2\\), so any smaller fixed ball lies in the scaled interior.</p><p>Finally, use countably many graph patches to obtain the intrinsic full-measure set of good points on each retained cell.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Either \\( T_pS+iT_pS=T_pV \\) outside an intrinsic null set of \\(S\\), or \\(S\\) is contained in a proper complex algebraic subset of \\(V\\).", "statement": "<p>**Complex rank of a boundary cell; original Lemma 3.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^N\\) be an irreducible complex affine variety of complex dimension \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(S\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be a connected real-analytic semialgebraic cell.</p><p class=\"li\">The cell need not be a boundary cell for this lemma.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Either\n\\[\nT_pS+iT_pS=T_pV\n\\]\noutside an intrinsic null set of \\(S\\), or \\(S\\) is contained in a proper complex algebraic subset of \\(V\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(d=\\dim_{\\mathbb R}S\\), and write the ambient complex coordinate functions of an algebraic analytic parametrization of \\(S\\) as\n\\[\nq_1(s),\\ldots,q_N(s),\n\\qquad s=(s_1,\\ldots,s_d).\n\\]\nComplexify the parameters. These germs belong to a common finite algebraic extension of \\(\\mathbb C(s_1,\\ldots,s_d)\\). In characteristic zero, the dimension of the span of their differentials is\n\\[\n\\operatorname{trdeg}_{\\mathbb C}\n\\mathbb C(q_1,\\ldots,q_N).\n\\]\nIndeed, a transcendence basis among the \\(q_j\\) has independent differentials, as seen by extending it to a transcendence basis of the ambient field; all remaining \\(q_j\\) are separably algebraic over it and add no independent differentials.</p><p>This number is also the generic complex rank of the complexified parametrization and the dimension of its complex Zariski closure. The latter lies in \\(V\\).</p><p>If the rank is less than \\(n\\), a polynomial vanishing on that closure but not identically on \\(V\\) vanishes on an open patch of \\(S\\), hence on all of \\(S\\) by real-analytic continuation. If the rank is \\(n\\), its failure locus is defined locally by nontrivial analytic minors and has intrinsic measure zero. The complex image of the differential is exactly \\(T_pS+iT_pS\\), which proves the alternative.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a null set \\(E\\subset B\\) such that, for every \\(s_*\\in B\\setminus E\\), there are a neighborhood \\(B_*\\) of \\(s_*\\), an integer \\(l\\geq1\\), a number \\(\\varepsilon>0\\), and a real-analytic function …", "statement": "<p>**Ramification with parameters; original Lemma 3.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, replace the boundary setting of Proposition 3 by a semialgebraic open set \\(B\\subset\\mathbb R^d\\), a number \\(t_0&gt;0\\), and a bounded semialgebraic function\n\\[\nb:B\\times(0,t_0)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a null set \\(E\\subset B\\) such that, for every \\(s_*\\in B\\setminus E\\), there are a neighborhood \\(B_*\\) of \\(s_*\\), an integer \\(l\\geq1\\), a number \\(\\varepsilon&gt;0\\), and a real-analytic function\n\\[\n\\widetilde b:B_*\\times(-\\varepsilon,\\varepsilon)\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\n\\]\nsatisfying\n\\[\nb(s,u^l)=\\widetilde b(s,u)\n\\quad(s\\in B_*,\\ 0&lt;u&lt;\\varepsilon).\n\\]</p><p>In particular, \\(b(s,t)\\) has a limit as \\((s,t)\\to(s_*,0)\\) with \\(t&gt;0\\), without restriction on the relative rates of approach.</p><p>Finitely many such functions can use a common ramification and neighborhood. For countably many functions there is a common full-measure set of good points, with the conclusion applied separately to each function; no common ramification or neighborhood is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use cylindrical decomposition with \\(s\\) preceding \\(t\\). Away from a lower-dimensional subset of \\(B\\), the region immediately above \\(t=0\\) lies in a cell on which \\(b\\) is an algebraic analytic branch. Locally at such an \\(s_*\\), shrink to a rectangle\n\\[\nB_*\\times(0,\\varepsilon_0)\n\\]\non which this holds.</p><p>Choose a square-free polynomial equation\n\\[\nR(s,t,b(s,t))=0\n\\]\nwith polynomial coefficients in \\((s,t)\\) and nonzero leading coefficient and discriminant in the last variable. Remove the zero sets of the first nonzero \\(t\\)-coefficients of these latter two polynomials. After shrinking again, each is a power of \\(t\\) times a nonvanishing holomorphic function on a complex \\(s\\)-polydisc times a complex \\(t\\)-disc.</p><p>For \\(t\\neq0\\), the roots therefore form a finite unramified covering of this product with the \\(t\\)-origin deleted. The \\(s\\)-polydisc is simply connected, so its monodromy is generated by one finite permutation around \\(t=0\\). The substitution \\(t=u^l\\) kills this permutation for some \\(l\\). The chosen branch becomes holomorphic for \\(u\\neq0\\).</p><p>The polynomial root bound, applied after division by the leading coefficient, bounds this branch by \\(C|u|^{-M}\\) on a smaller complex polydisc, for some finite \\(M\\). Thus it has at worst a pole at \\(u=0\\), whose negative Laurent coefficients are holomorphic in \\(s\\). Boundedness of the original function for real \\(s\\) and positive \\(u\\) makes those coefficients vanish for every real \\(s\\) in the rectangle. They vanish identically by holomorphic uniqueness. The branch consequently extends holomorphically through \\(u=0\\), giving the stated real-analytic extension.</p><p>There are only finitely many decomposition branches and exceptional polynomial zero sets. This proves the assertion outside a null set. For finitely many functions, take a common multiple of their exponents and shrink the neighborhoods. For countably many, discard the union of their exceptional null sets; the individual conclusions suffice.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are a boundary cell \\(S\\) in the first alternative of Proposition 3, a good point \\(p\\in S\\), and \\( k=2n-\\dim_{\\mathbb R}S \\) entire functions \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_k\\) on \\(\\mathbb C^N\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>**A good supported point; original Proposition 3.4.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V\\subset\\mathbb C^N\\) be irreducible of complex dimension \\(n\\geq1\\), and let \\(U\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be a nonempty connected semialgebraic bounded open subset.</p><p class=\"li\">Use a finite boundary-cell decomposition as in Proposition 3.</p><p class=\"li\">A good point means a full-complex-rank point of a retained cell at which the analytic interior graphs and joint normal-offset distance limits of Proposition 3 both hold.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are a boundary cell \\(S\\) in the first alternative of Proposition 3, a good point \\(p\\in S\\), and\n\\[\nk=2n-\\dim_{\\mathbb R}S\n\\]\nentire functions \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_k\\) on \\(\\mathbb C^N\\) such that\n\\[\nh_j(p)=1,\\qquad\n|h_j(q)|\\leq\\exp(-|q-p|^2)\n\\quad(q\\in\\overline U),\\qquad 1\\leq j\\leq k.\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThe real covectors\n\\[\nd\\log|h_j|\\big|_{T_pV}\n\\]\nare linearly independent and annihilate \\(T_pS\\).</p><p>Consequently, in local holomorphic coordinates\n\\[\n(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^{n-k}\\times\\mathbb C^k\n\\]\ncentered at \\(p\\) with \\(T_pS=\\{\\operatorname{Im}w=0\\}\\), the logarithmic differentials have the form\n\\[\nd\\log h_j(p)=i\\beta_j\\,dw,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\beta_1,\\ldots,\\beta_k\\) are linearly independent real rows and each local logarithm has value zero at \\(p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>We vary the real linear term in the weighted maximum used in the smoothness proof. We seek one good point at which the maximum is attained for a positive-measure set of linear terms, measured within the set of all terms making that point critical on its boundary cell. The corresponding conormals will provide \\(k\\) independent supports.</p><p>Choose a polynomial \\(P\\) vanishing on \\(V_{\\mathrm{sing}}\\) and on the proper complex algebraic subsets containing all cells in the second alternative of Proposition 3, but not identically on \\(V\\). Such a polynomial exists because the union is finite and \\(V\\) is irreducible.</p><p>For \\(a\\in\\mathbb C^N\\), maximize on \\(\\overline U\\) the upper-semicontinuous function\n\\[\n\\Phi_a(q)=\\log|P(q)|+|q|^2+\\operatorname{Re}(a\\cdot q),\n\\]\nwith value \\(-\\infty\\) where \\(P=0\\). Its maximum \\(M(a)\\) is finite, because \\(P\\) is not identically zero on the open set \\(U\\). Every maximizer has \\(P\\neq0\\), lies in \\(V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\), and belongs to \\(\\partial U\\): on the smooth locus with \\(P\\neq0\\), strict plurisubharmonicity forbids an interior maximum. Thus every maximizer lies on a retained boundary cell.</p><p>Fix one retained cell \\(S\\), of real dimension \\(d\\), and restrict to\n\\[\nS^\\circ=S\\cap\\{P\\neq0\\}.\n\\]\nIts critical-pair space is\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_S\n=\n\\{(p,a)\\in S^\\circ\\times\\mathbb C^N:\nd(\\Phi_a|_S)_p=0\\}.\n\\]\nAmbient real linear functionals restrict onto \\(T_p^*S\\), so these are \\(d\\) independent real affine equations on the \\(2N\\) real coordinates of \\(a\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_S\\longrightarrow S^\\circ\n\\]\nis a smooth affine bundle with fiber dimension \\(2N-d\\) and total dimension \\(2N\\).</p><p>The subset \\(\\mathcal M_S\\subset\\mathcal E_S\\) of actual maximizers is Borel. Indeed, \\(M(a)\\) is Lipschitz in \\(a\\), since\n\\[\n|M(a)-M(a')|\n\\leq\n\\left(\\sup_{q\\in\\overline U}|q|\\right)|a-a'|,\n\\]\nand \\(\\mathcal M_S\\) is defined by the continuous equality \\(\\Phi_a(p)=M(a)\\) on \\(\\mathcal E_S\\).</p><p>Projection \\(\\mathcal E_S\\to\\mathbb C^N\\) is a smooth map between manifolds of the same real dimension and sends null sets to null sets: in countably many relatively compact coordinate charts it is Lipschitz. Its restrictions to the finitely many sets \\(\\mathcal M_S\\) cover all of \\(\\mathbb C^N\\). Hence at least one \\(\\mathcal M_S\\) has positive measure in \\(\\mathcal E_S\\).</p><p>In local bundle coordinates, the points lying over the nongood null subset of \\(S\\) form a null set, by Fubini. Applying Fubini again to \\(\\mathcal M_S\\), in countably many bounded bundle charts, gives a good point \\(p\\) for which the set of actual maximizing parameters in the critical fiber has positive \\((2N-d)\\)-dimensional measure. Fix this \\(p\\) and write that subset as \\(A_p\\).</p><p>Each \\(a\\in A_p\\) supplies the entire function\n\\[\nh_a(q)\n=\n\\frac{P(q)}{P(p)}\n\\exp\\bigl((2\\overline p+a)\\cdot(q-p)\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe inequality \\(\\Phi_a(q)\\leq\\Phi_a(p)\\) gives\n\\[\n\\log|h_a(q)|\n\\leq\n|p|^2-|q|^2\n+2\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(\\overline p\\cdot(q-p)\\bigr)\n=\n-|q-p|^2,\n\\]\nwith the desired modulus inequality also at \\(P(q)=0\\).</p><p>The affine map from the full critical fiber to the conormal space\n\\[\na\\longmapsto\nd\\log|h_a|\\big|_{T_pV}\n\\in\nN_p^*S\n:=\n\\{\\lambda\\in T_p^*V:\\lambda|_{T_pS}=0\\}\n\\]\nis surjective. Indeed, on \\(T_pV\\),\n\\[\nd\\log|h_a|_p\n=\nd(\\log|P|+|q|^2)_p+\\operatorname{Re}(a\\cdot dq).\n\\]\nFor any \\(\\lambda\\in N_p^*S\\), extend\n\\[\n\\lambda-d(\\log|P|+|q|^2)_p\n\\]\nto an ambient real linear functional \\(\\operatorname{Re}(a\\cdot dq)\\). The resulting total differential is \\(\\lambda\\), so its restriction to \\(T_pS\\) vanishes and \\(a\\) belongs to the critical fiber.</p><p>Since \\(\\dim_{\\mathbb R}N_p^*S=k\\), the inverse image of any proper linear subspace of \\(N_p^*S\\) has measure zero in the critical fiber. The positive-measure set \\(A_p\\) cannot be contained in such an inverse image. Selecting successively outside the span already chosen gives \\(k\\) independent conormals and corresponding functions \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_k\\).</p><p>Finally, choose the local branches of \\(\\log h_j\\) with value zero at \\(p\\). Their real differentials vanish on\n\\[\n\\mathbb C^{n-k}\\times\\mathbb R^k=T_pS.\n\\]\nComplex linearity forces the \\(dz\\) coefficients to be zero and the \\(dw\\) coefficients to be purely imaginary. Independence of the real conormals is exactly independence of the resulting real rows \\(\\beta_j\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\( F\\neq\\mathbb R^k. \\)", "statement": "<p>**Noncollapse; original Proposition 4.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) be as in Proposition 4, and suppose additionally that \\(U\\) has the compact covering action considered in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the cell \\(S\\), good supported point \\(p\\), and old coordinates\n\\[\n(z_0,w_0)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k,\n\\qquad m+k=n,\n\\]\nsupplied by Propositions 3 and 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n\\[\nS:\\operatorname{Im}w_0=\\varphi(s),\n\\qquad\ns=(z_0,\\operatorname{Re}w_0),\n\\qquad\ns_*=s(p),\n\\]\nand use the fibers\n\\[\nA_s=\n\\{v\\in\\mathbb R^k:\n|v|&lt;\\rho,\\\n(z_0,x_0+i(\\varphi(s)+v))\\in U\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(d_*\\) be the joint limit of\n\\[\n\\min\\{1,\\operatorname{dist}(v,\\mathbb R^k\\setminus t^{-1}A_s)\\}\n\\]\nat \\((s_*,0)\\), and put \\(F=\\{d_*=0\\}\\), as in Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nF\\neq\\mathbb R^k.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep the old coordinates \\((z_0,w_0)\\) and fix the complex slice \\(z_0=z_0(p)\\). The graph of \\(S\\) in this slice is totally real and real analytic. Its parametrization by \\(\\operatorname{Re}w_0\\) therefore has invertible complexified derivative, and its complexification provides a local biholomorphic change of coordinates sending this graph to \\(\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(p\\) to \\(0\\).</p><p>Restrict the analytic interior graphs from Proposition 3 to this slice. In the new coordinates their real-part projection is the identity at \\(u=0\\). The real-analytic implicit function theorem consequently writes them, on a common neighborhood, as\n\\[\nX+if_u(X),\\qquad f_0(X)=0.\n\\]\nThe identity at \\(u=0\\) holds for every \\(X\\) in this neighborhood, not just for \\(X=0\\). For \\(u&gt;0\\), these graphs lie in the slice of \\(U\\).</p><p>The continuity principle of Proposition 2 in the ambient smooth chart applies to discs in this fixed slice and is unchanged by its biholomorphic coordinate change. Lemma 5.1 now gives slice balls of radii \\(ct\\) with centers \\(O(t)\\) from \\(p\\).</p><p>Return to the \\(w_0\\)-coordinates. Uniform bounds for the coordinate change and its inverse, after one fixed shrinking, give constants \\(c',C'&gt;0\\) and centers \\(w_t\\) such that\n\\[\nB(w_t,c't)\n\\subset\n\\{w_0:(z_0(p),w_0)\\in U\\},\n\\qquad\n|w_t-w_0(p)|\\leq C't.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\ns_t=(z_0(p),\\operatorname{Re}w_t),\n\\qquad\nv_t=\\operatorname{Im}w_t-\\varphi(s_t).\n\\]\nIntersecting each complex ball with the real affine slice\n\\(\\operatorname{Re}w_0=\\operatorname{Re}w_t\\) gives\n\\[\nB(v_t,c't)\\subset A_{s_t},\n\\qquad s_t\\to s_*,\n\\qquad |v_t|=O(t).\n\\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nThe last estimate uses the smoothness of \\(\\varphi\\) and the fact that \\(p\\) lies on its graph.</p><p>Choose a sequence \\(t_j\\downarrow0\\) for which \\(v_{t_j}/t_j\\to v\\). Equation (4.9) implies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}\n\\bigl(v,\\mathbb R^k\\setminus t_j^{-1}A_{s_{t_j}}\\bigr)\n\\geq\nc'-|v-v_{t_j}/t_j|\n\\geq c'/2\n\\]\nfor large \\(j\\). If \\(v\\) belonged to \\(F\\), the moving-parameter excluded-offset conclusion of Proposition 3 would force this distance to tend to zero. Thus \\(v\\notin F\\), as required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There are constants \\(c,C,t_0>0\\) and points \\(a_t\\in\\mathbb C^k\\), for \\(0<t<t_0\\), such that \\( |a_t|\\leq Ct,\\qquad B(a_t,ct)\\subset\\Omega. \\) The discs used to obtain these balls stay in an arbitrarily small fixed …", "statement": "<p>**Interior balls from approaching graphs; original Lemma 4.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, replace the affine-domain setting of Proposition 5 by the following local setting in \\(\\mathbb C^k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega\\) be open in a neighborhood of \\(0\\in\\mathbb C^k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that the continuity principle of Proposition 2 holds for continuous deformations of closed analytic discs contained in that neighborhood.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(f(X,u)\\) is an \\(\\mathbb R^k\\)-valued real-analytic function for real \\(X\\) near \\(0\\) and real \\(u\\) near \\(0\\), that \\(f(X,0)=0\\), and that\n\\[\nX+if(X,u)\\in\\Omega\n\\]\nfor all such \\(X\\) and all sufficiently small \\(u&gt;0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are constants \\(c,C,t_0&gt;0\\) and points \\(a_t\\in\\mathbb C^k\\), for \\(0&lt;t&lt;t_0\\), such that\n\\[\n|a_t|\\leq Ct,\\qquad B(a_t,ct)\\subset\\Omega.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nThe discs used to obtain these balls stay in an arbitrarily small fixed neighborhood of \\(0\\) after the graph neighborhood is restricted.</p>", "proof": "<p>Write \\(f_u(X)=f(X,u)\\). Choose nonnegative smooth functions \\(\\lambda_0\\) and \\(\\eta\\) on the unit circle, both zero on a fixed arc about \\(1\\), with \\(\\eta\\not\\equiv0\\) and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{supp}\\eta\\Subset\\{\\lambda_0&gt;0\\}.\n\\]\nSet \\(\\lambda=\\alpha\\lambda_0\\), where the positive constant \\(\\alpha\\) will be fixed sufficiently small.</p><p>For parameters \\(b,e\\in\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(\\delta\\geq0\\), seek a holomorphic disc \\(A_{b,e,\\delta}\\) whose boundary values \\(X+iY\\) satisfy\n\\[\nX(0)=b,\\qquad\nY(\\theta)\n=\nf_{\\lambda(\\theta)+\\delta}(X(\\theta))+\\eta(\\theta)e.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nHere \\(X(0)\\) means the value at the boundary angle \\(\\theta=0\\), not the value at the center of the disc.</p><p>When \\(\\delta=0\\), the boundary arc about \\(1\\) maps into \\(\\mathbb R^k\\), with endpoint \\(A_{b,e,0}(1)=b\\). Varying \\(b\\) moves that endpoint in the real directions; the perturbation \\(\\eta e\\), supported away from \\(1\\), will change the inward radial derivative in all imaginary directions. For \\(B\\in\\mathbb R^k\\), evaluation at \\(1-t\\) with \\(b=tB\\) will therefore give independent displacements of order \\(t\\) in all \\(2k\\) real directions. We first prove this derivative assertion and then use a positive \\(\\delta\\) to put the evaluated discs inside \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=H^2(S^1,\\mathbb R^k)\n\\]\nbe the real Sobolev space of functions with two square-integrable derivatives. The Fourier-series estimate\n\\[\n\\sum_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}\n\\frac{j^2}{(1+j^2)^2}&lt;\\infty\n\\]\ngives the continuous embedding \\(\\mathcal H\\hookrightarrow C^1(S^1)\\). This space is a Banach algebra under multiplication.</p><p>Let \\(T\\) be the bounded conjugation operator which recovers the real part of a holomorphic boundary value from its imaginary part, with real part of mean zero, and put\n\\[\n\\mathcal T Y=TY-(TY)(0).\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathcal T\\) is bounded on \\(\\mathcal H\\), and (4.2) is equivalent to\n\\[\nX=b+\\mathcal T\\bigl(f_{\\lambda+\\delta}(X)+\\eta e\\bigr).\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p><p>The identity \\(f(X,0)=0\\) gives a real-analytic factorization\n\\[\nf(X,u)=u\\widetilde f(X,u).\n\\]\nOn a fixed small \\(\\mathcal H\\)-ball of functions \\(X\\), the product rule through two derivatives, the embedding into \\(C^1\\), and bounded derivatives of \\(\\widetilde f\\) give\n\\[\n\\|f_{\\lambda+\\delta}(X)\\|_{\\mathcal H}\n+\n\\|D_X[f_{\\lambda+\\delta}(X)]\\|_{\\mathcal L(\\mathcal H)}\n\\leq C_0(\\alpha+|\\delta|).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nBoth expressions contain the factor \\(\\lambda+\\delta\\); derivatives falling on that factor are also \\(O(\\alpha+|\\delta|)\\). The remaining composition and multiplication operators are uniformly bounded on the chosen ball.</p><p>For sufficiently small \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(|\\delta|\\), the Lipschitz constant in (4.3) is less than \\(1/2\\). On common sufficiently small \\(b,e\\) neighborhoods, its right side maps the chosen ball into itself. The contraction theorem gives a unique solution \\(X\\).</p><p>Smooth composition on \\(H^2\\), or its direct proof by the same product estimates, shows that this equation is smooth in \\(X,b,e,\\delta\\). Its \\(X\\)-linearization is the identity minus an operator of norm less than \\(1/2\\). The implicit function theorem therefore gives smooth parameter dependence in \\(\\mathcal H\\). In particular, \\(\\partial_eX\\) is uniformly bounded as \\(\\alpha\\) decreases; it need not be small.</p><p>The boundary function \\(X+iY\\) extends holomorphically to the disc and continuously to its closure. All these conclusions hold also with \\(\\lambda\\) replaced by \\(\\sigma\\lambda\\), \\(0\\leq\\sigma\\leq1\\). Shrinking the fixed \\(\\mathcal H\\)-ball keeps every disc in the prescribed coordinate neighborhood, by the boundary bound and the maximum principle.</p><p>For \\(\\delta=0\\), the function \\(Y\\) vanishes on the fixed arc about \\(\\theta=0\\). Hence \\(A_{b,e,0}(1)=b\\), and reflection across that arc shows that the radial derivative there is purely imaginary. More explicitly, with \\(Y=Y_{b,e,0}\\), the Schwarz integral gives, near \\(\\zeta=1\\),\n\\[\nA_{b,e,0}(\\zeta)-b\n=\n\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\n\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\n\\left(\n\\frac{e^{i\\theta}+\\zeta}{e^{i\\theta}-\\zeta}\n-\n\\frac{e^{i\\theta}+1}{e^{i\\theta}-1}\n\\right)Y(\\theta)\\,d\\theta.\n\\]\nThere is no singularity on the support of \\(Y\\). Expanding the kernel at \\(\\zeta=1\\) gives\n\\[\nA_{b,e,0}(1-t)=b+itD(b,e)+O(t^2),\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nD(b,e)\n=\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\n\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\n\\frac{2Y_{b,e,0}(\\theta)}{|1-e^{i\\theta}|^2}\\,d\\theta.\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nThe remainder is uniform with its first derivatives in \\(b,e\\) on a fixed smaller parameter neighborhood. This follows from the same integral formula and the smooth \\(\\mathcal H\\)-dependence proved above.</p><p>Differentiating (4.6) in \\(e\\) gives\n\\[\n\\partial_eD(b,e)=c_\\eta I+R_\\alpha(b,e),\n\\qquad\nc_\\eta\n=\n\\frac1{2\\pi}\n\\int_{-\\pi}^{\\pi}\n\\frac{2\\eta(\\theta)}{|1-e^{i\\theta}|^2}\\,d\\theta&gt;0.\n\\]\nThe remainder contains\n\\[\n\\partial_Xf_{\\lambda(\\theta)}(X)\\,\\partial_eX.\n\\]\nThe first factor is \\(O(\\alpha)\\) and the second is uniformly bounded. Their product vanishes on the arc where \\(\\lambda=0\\), so the integral has a uniformly bounded kernel on its support. Thus\n\\[\n\\|R_\\alpha\\|\\leq C_1\\alpha.\n\\]\nThe positive constant \\(c_\\eta\\) is independent of \\(\\alpha\\). Fix \\(\\alpha\\) sufficiently small that \\(\\partial_eD(0,0)\\) is invertible, and only then fix the smaller \\(b,e,\\delta\\) parameter neighborhoods ensuring all the conclusions above.</p><p>For this fixed positive \\(\\alpha\\), on \\(\\operatorname{supp}\\eta\\) the function \\(\\lambda\\) has a positive minimum. As \\(X\\) ranges in a fixed smaller closed real ball compactly contained in the graph chart, \\(\\theta\\) ranges in this support, and \\(\\delta\\) ranges in a sufficiently small closed interval \\([0,\\delta_0]\\), the points\n\\[\nX+if_{\\lambda(\\theta)+\\delta}(X)\n\\]\nform a compact subset of \\(\\Omega\\). Consequently, after reducing the allowed size of \\(e\\), their displacements by \\(i\\eta(\\theta)e\\) still belong to \\(\\Omega\\), uniformly for \\(0\\leq\\delta\\leq\\delta_0\\). Outside this support there is no displacement. Thus for every \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), all boundary values in (4.2) are in \\(\\Omega\\).</p><p>For a fixed \\(\\delta&gt;0\\), set \\(e=0\\) and increase \\(\\sigma\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(1\\) in \\(\\sigma\\lambda\\). At \\(\\sigma=0\\), the solution is the constant disc \\(b+if_\\delta(b)\\). All boundary values during this deformation lie on interior graphs, so the continuity principle puts every disc in \\(\\Omega\\). Next increase the displacement from \\(0\\) to \\(e\\), now with \\(\\lambda\\) fixed. The same principle applies by the preceding compact interior bound. We have proved\n\\[\nA_{b,e,\\delta}(\\overline{\\mathbb D})\\subset\\Omega\n\\qquad(\\delta&gt;0).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nNo inclusion is claimed for \\(\\delta=0\\). The final radius allowed for \\(e\\) may depend on the fixed \\(\\alpha\\). No uniform lower bound as \\(\\alpha\\downarrow0\\) is needed: the included-ball constants below are fixed only after these choices.</p><p>For real parameters \\((B,e)\\) in a fixed small ball in \\(\\mathbb R^{2k}\\), define\n\\[\n\\Psi_t^0(B,e)=t^{-1}A_{tB,e,0}(1-t).\n\\]\nEquation (4.5) gives convergence in \\(C^1\\) on that ball:\n\\[\n\\Psi_t^0(B,e)\n\\longrightarrow\n\\Psi_0(B,e):=B+iD(0,e).\n\\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nThe real differential \\(J=D\\Psi_0(0,0)\\) is invertible.</p><p>Smooth parameter dependence and bounded evaluation on the closed disc also give, on a fixed compact parameter set,\n\\[\n\\|A_{b,e,\\delta}-A_{b,e,0}\\|_\n{C^1((b,e);C^0(\\overline{\\mathbb D}))}\n\\leq C_2\\delta.\n\\]\nIndeed, integrate the uniformly bounded derivatives\n\\[\n\\partial_\\delta A,\\qquad\n\\partial_b\\partial_\\delta A,\\qquad\n\\partial_e\\partial_\\delta A\n\\]\nover \\([0,\\delta]\\); their bounds in \\(H^2\\) control the displayed norm.</p><p>Take the explicit positive regularization \\(\\delta(t)=t^2\\). The maps\n\\[\n\\Psi_t(B,e)=t^{-1}A_{tB,e,t^2}(1-t)\n\\]\ndiffer from \\(\\Psi_t^0\\) by \\(O(t)\\) in \\(C^1\\): the value and \\(e\\)-derivative errors are \\(O(\\delta/t)\\), while the \\(B\\)-derivative error is \\(O(\\delta)\\). They therefore satisfy the same limit (4.8). By (4.7), their images lie in \\(t^{-1}\\Omega\\).</p><p>This \\(C^1\\) convergence gives included balls, not merely interior limit points. Choose a closed parameter ball \\(\\overline B(0,r)\\) so small that\n\\[\n\\|J^{-1}(D\\Psi_0-J)\\|&lt;\\frac14\n\\]\nthere. For small \\(t\\), the corresponding bound for \\(\\Psi_t\\) is less than \\(1/2\\). If\n\\[\n|y-\\Psi_t(0)|&lt;\\frac{r}{2\\|J^{-1}\\|},\n\\]\nthe map\n\\[\nx\\longmapsto x-J^{-1}(\\Psi_t(x)-y)\n\\]\nis a contraction of \\(\\overline B(0,r)\\) into itself. Its fixed point proves that \\(y\\) is in the image of \\(\\Psi_t\\). These images thus contain balls of one fixed positive radius, centered at the bounded points \\(\\Psi_t(0)\\). Multiplication by \\(t\\) proves (4.1).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are an invertible real linear map \\(L:\\mathbb R^k\\to\\mathbb R^k\\) and a vector \\(Q\\) of real quadratic forms on \\(\\mathbb C^m\\) such that, with …", "statement": "<p>**Two-sided convergence of the scaled sets; original Proposition 5.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\) satisfy the positive-dimensional setting of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix the good supported point \\(p\\), boundary cell \\(S\\), and old graph coordinates \\((z_0,w_0)\\) of Propositions 3 and 4.</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\ns=(z_0,\\operatorname{Re}w_0),\\qquad\nv=\\operatorname{Im}w_0-\\varphi(s),\n\\]\nand let \\(F\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) be the closed excluded cone obtained from the joint normal-offset distance limits at \\(p\\). By Proposition 5, \\(F\\neq\\mathbb R^k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose translated, complex-linearly adjusted holomorphic coordinates \\((z,w)\\) centered at \\(p\\) such that\n\\[\nT_pS=\\{\\operatorname{Im}w=0\\},\n\\qquad\n(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k,\n\\qquad m=n-k.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Phi\\) be the inverse coordinate chart, with \\(\\Phi(0)=p\\), defined onto a neighborhood \\(\\Omega\\subset V_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\n\\delta_t(Z,W)=(\\sqrt t\\,Z,tW),\n\\qquad\nD_t=\\delta_t^{-1}\\bigl(\\Phi^{-1}(U\\cap\\Omega)\\bigr)\n\\quad(t&gt;0).\n\\]\nEvery fixed compact subset of \\(\\mathbb C^n\\) lies in the domain of \\(\\Phi\\circ\\delta_t\\) for all sufficiently small \\(t\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are an invertible real linear map \\(L:\\mathbb R^k\\to\\mathbb R^k\\) and a vector \\(Q\\) of real quadratic forms on \\(\\mathbb C^m\\) such that, with\n\\[\nR(Z,W)=L(\\operatorname{Im}W)-Q(Z),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal O=\\{(Z,W):R(Z,W)\\notin F\\},\n\\]\nthe following hold:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Every compact subset of \\(\\mathcal O\\) is contained in \\(D_t\\) for all sufficiently small \\(t&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li num\">2. For every \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb C^n\\) with \\(R(\\xi)\\in F\\) and every sequence \\(t_j\\downarrow0\\), there are \\(\\xi_j\\notin D_{t_j}\\) such that \\(\\xi_j\\to\\xi\\). These excluded points can be chosen within the scaled coordinate charts.</p><p>Moreover, choose any connected component\n\\[\nC\\subset L^{-1}(\\mathbb R^k\\setminus F),\n\\]\nand put\n\\[\nP_2=L^{-1}Q,\\qquad\nD_0=\\{(Z,W):\\operatorname{Im}W-P_2(Z)\\in C\\}.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nThen \\(C\\) is nonempty, connected, open, and invariant under every positive dilation, and \\(D_0\\) is exactly one connected component of \\(\\mathcal O\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Regard the old offset \\(v\\) as a real-analytic function of the new coordinates. Its differential has kernel \\(T_pS\\), so\n\\[\ndv_0(z,w)=L(\\operatorname{Im}w)\n\\]\nwith \\(L\\) invertible. Taylor expansion therefore gives\n\\[\nR_t(Z,W)\n:=\nt^{-1}v\\bigl(\\Phi(\\sqrt t\\,Z,tW)\\bigr)\n\\longrightarrow\nL(\\operatorname{Im}W)-Q(Z)\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nuniformly on compact sets. Here \\(-Q\\) is the part of the quadratic Taylor term involving only \\(z\\); the mixed and normal quadratic terms disappear after division by \\(t\\). The old parameter\n\\[\ns_t(Z,W)=s\\bigl(\\Phi(\\sqrt t\\,Z,tW)\\bigr)\n\\]\ntends uniformly on compact sets to \\(s_*\\).</p><p>The distance convergence in Proposition 3 says that\n\\[\nd(s,t;u)\n=\n\\min\\{1,\\operatorname{dist}(u,\\mathbb R^k\\setminus t^{-1}A_s)\\}\n\\longrightarrow d_*(u)\n\\]\nas \\((s,t)\\to(s_*,0^+)\\), locally uniformly in \\(u\\), with \\(F=\\{d_*=0\\}\\). It applies to moving vectors as well as to moving parameters.</p><p>If assertion 1 failed, a compactness argument would give\n\\[\n\\xi_j\\to\\xi\\in\\mathcal O,\\qquad\nt_j\\downarrow0,\\qquad\n\\xi_j\\notin D_{t_j}.\n\\]\nThe corresponding offsets \\(R_{t_j}(\\xi_j)\\) are excluded from\n\\[\nt_j^{-1}A_{s_{t_j}(\\xi_j)}.\n\\]\nTheir distances to the excluded sets are thus zero, whereas their limit has distance value \\(d_*(R(\\xi))&gt;0\\). This is a contradiction.</p><p>For assertion 2, set\n\\[\nq_j=\\Phi(\\delta_{t_j}\\xi),\\qquad s_j=s(q_j).\n\\]\nSince \\(R_{t_j}(\\xi)\\to R(\\xi)\\in F\\), the same joint convergence supplies\n\\[\nu_j\\in\\mathbb R^k\\setminus t_j^{-1}A_{s_j},\n\\qquad\nu_j-R_{t_j}(\\xi)\\to0.\n\\]\nIn the old chart change only the imaginary normal coordinate, replacing the offset \\(v(q_j)\\) by \\(t_ju_j\\). The resulting point \\(q_j'\\) is outside \\(U\\), has the same \\(s_j\\), and differs from \\(q_j\\) by \\(o(t_j)\\) in old coordinates. It remains in \\(\\Omega\\) for large \\(j\\). The fixed coordinate change is smooth, so the change is also \\(o(t_j)\\) in the new coordinates. Consequently,\n\\[\n\\xi_j:=\\delta_{t_j}^{-1}\\Phi^{-1}(q_j')\n\\longrightarrow\\xi,\n\\qquad\n\\xi_j\\notin D_{t_j}.\n\\]\nIndeed, an \\(o(t_j)\\) change becomes \\(o(\\sqrt{t_j})\\) in the \\(Z\\) coordinates and \\(o(1)\\) in the \\(W\\) coordinates. All points used here approach \\(p\\), so the local cutoffs in the definition of \\(A_s\\) do not affect the argument.</p><p>Finally, \\(L^{-1}(\\mathbb R^k\\setminus F)\\) is a nonempty open cone. Every positive dilation preserves each connected component \\(C\\): for \\(a\\in C\\), the path \\(r\\mapsto ra\\) stays in the complement cone and connects \\(a\\) to any positive multiple.</p><p>The real coordinate change\n\\[\n(Z,W)\\longmapsto\n(Z,\\operatorname{Re}W,\\operatorname{Im}W-P_2(Z))\n\\]\nidentifies \\(D_0\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb R^k\\times C.\n\\]\nThus \\(D_0\\) is connected and is precisely one connected component of \\(\\mathcal O\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are integers \\( 1\\leq k\\leq n,\\qquad m=n-k, \\) a nonempty connected open cone \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) invariant under every positive dilation, and a vector \\(H\\) of Hermitian forms on \\(\\mathbb C^m\\) such that …", "statement": "<p>**Quadratic model; original Theorem 5.2, used as an intermediate result for Theorem 1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\) satisfy the assumptions of Theorem 1 and have positive complex dimension \\(n\\).</p><p class=\"li\">By Proposition 1, regard \\(U\\) as a smooth domain in the smooth locus of an irreducible affine variety.</p><p class=\"li\">A vector of Hermitian forms \\(H\\) is complex linear in its first argument; \\(H(z,z)\\in\\mathbb R^k\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are integers\n\\[\n1\\leq k\\leq n,\\qquad m=n-k,\n\\]\na nonempty connected open cone \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) invariant under every positive dilation, and a vector \\(H\\) of Hermitian forms on \\(\\mathbb C^m\\) such that \\(U\\) is biholomorphic to\n\\[\nD=\n\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k:\n\\operatorname{Im}w-H(z,z)\\in C\\}.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nThere are independent real linear functionals\n\\[\n\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_k:\\mathbb R^k\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\n\\]\nand a constant \\(c&gt;0\\) such that\n\\[\n\\ell_a(y)&gt;0\\quad(y\\in C),\n\\qquad\n\\ell_a(H(z,z))\\geq c\\|z\\|^2\n\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb C^m),\n\\qquad 1\\leq a\\leq k.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nThe original action transfers to a properly discontinuous action on \\(D\\) with compact quotient. No freeness assumption is required.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Propositions 3–6 to fix \\(p,S,k,m\\), the inverse chart \\(\\Phi\\), the dilations \\(\\delta_t\\), the scaled domains \\(D_t\\), the independent supports \\(h_1,\\ldots,h_k\\), and one component\n\\[\nD_0=\\{(Z,W):\\operatorname{Im}W-P_2(Z)\\in C\\}\n\\]\nof the open limiting set \\(\\mathcal O\\). Proposition 5 guarantees that the complement cone from which \\(C\\) is chosen is nonempty. We first prove \\(U\\simeq D_0\\), and then remove pure quadratic terms and pass the support inequalities to the limit.</p><p>Choose \\(a_0\\in C\\), and write\n\\[\nb_0=(0,ia_0)\\in D_0.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 6, for a sufficiently small sequence \\(t_j\\downarrow0\\), the points\n\\[\np_j=\\Phi(0,it_ja_0)\n\\]\nbelong to \\(U\\). The compact covering set \\(K\\) of Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\gamma_j(k_j)=p_j,\\qquad\n\\gamma_j\\in\\Gamma,\\quad k_j\\in K.\n\\]\nAfter extraction, \\(k_j\\to k_*\\in K\\).</p><p>Each support \\(h_a\\) maps \\(U\\) into the unit disc, satisfies \\(h_a(p)=1\\), and has\n\\[\nh_a(p_j)=1+O(t_j).\n\\]\nFor a fixed compact subset \\(E\\subset U\\), the distances \\(d_U(q,k_j)\\) are bounded uniformly for \\(q\\in E\\). Schwarz contraction for the chain-of-discs distance implies that \\(h_a(\\gamma_jq)\\) is at uniformly bounded hyperbolic distance from \\(h_a(p_j)\\).</p><p>If two disc points \\(\\alpha,\\beta\\) have pseudohyperbolic distance at most \\(\\vartheta&lt;1\\), then\n\\[\n|\\beta-\\alpha|\n\\leq\n\\frac{\\vartheta(1-|\\alpha|^2)}{1-\\vartheta|\\alpha|}\n\\leq\n\\frac{2\\vartheta}{1-\\vartheta}(1-|\\alpha|).\n\\]\nIt follows that, uniformly on \\(E\\),\n\\[\nh_a(\\gamma_jq)=1+O_E(t_j),\n\\qquad 1\\leq a\\leq k.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nThe peak inequality (3.5) now gives\n\\[\n\\|\\gamma_jq-p\\|^2\n\\leq-\\log|h_a(\\gamma_jq)|\n=\nO_E(t_j).\n\\]\nIn particular, the images of \\(E\\) eventually lie in \\(\\Omega\\), and their new coordinates satisfy\n\\[\n(z,w)=O_E(\\sqrt{t_j}).\n\\]</p><p>The \\(k\\) supports sharpen the bound in all the normal directions. Their real logarithmic differentials annihilate\n\\[\nT_pS=\\{\\operatorname{Im}w=0\\}\n\\]\nand are independent. Therefore, taking local logarithms normalized by \\(\\log h_a(p)=0\\),\n\\[\nd(\\log h_a)_p=i\\ell_a(dw),\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_k\\) are independent real rows, extended complex linearly when applied to \\(w\\). A complex linear form whose real part vanishes on all \\(z\\) and real \\(w\\) has no \\(z\\) term and purely imaginary \\(w\\) coefficients.</p><p>Taylor expansion, the preliminary \\(O_E(\\sqrt{t_j})\\) bound, and (5.5) give\n\\[\ni\\ell_a(w)=O_E(t_j),\n\\qquad 1\\leq a\\leq k.\n\\]\nInvertibility of the matrix of these rows gives\n\\[\nw=O_E(t_j).\n\\]\nConsequently, the holomorphic maps\n\\[\nf_j=\\delta_{t_j}^{-1}\\circ\\Phi^{-1}\\circ\\gamma_j\n\\]\nare eventually defined on a neighborhood of every compact subset of \\(U\\) and are locally uniformly bounded. A diagonal normal-family extraction gives a holomorphic limit\n\\[\nf:U\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C^n.\n\\]</p><p>For each \\(j\\), the inverse coordinate map\n\\[\ng_j=\\gamma_j^{-1}\\circ\\Phi\\circ\\delta_{t_j}\n:\nD_{t_j}\\longrightarrow U\n\\]\nis defined on all of \\(D_{t_j}\\), and satisfies \\(g_j(b_0)=k_j\\). Every compact subset of \\(D_0\\) is eventually contained in its domain, by Proposition 6.</p><p>For every compact \\(E\\subset D_0\\), there is a compact \\(K_E\\subset U\\) containing \\(g_j(E)\\) for all sufficiently large \\(j\\). To see this, cover \\(E\\) by finitely many balls\n\\[\nB(x_\\nu,r_\\nu),\n\\qquad\n\\overline B(x_\\nu,2r_\\nu)\\subset D_0.\n\\]\nConnectedness and small balls along paths give a fixed finite disc chain from \\(b_0\\) to each \\(x_\\nu\\), with all closed disc images in \\(D_0\\). For \\(x\\in B(x_\\nu,r_\\nu)\\), append the disc\n\\[\n\\zeta\\longmapsto x_\\nu+2\\zeta(x-x_\\nu),\n\\]\nwhose parameters \\(0\\) and \\(1/2\\) join \\(x_\\nu\\) to \\(x\\).</p><p>All these chains lie in one compact subset of \\(D_0\\), and their total Poincaré lengths have a common bound. That compact subset is contained in \\(D_{t_j}\\) for all large \\(j\\). Composition with \\(g_j\\) therefore proves\n\\[\nd_U(g_j(x),k_j)\\leq M_E\n\\qquad(x\\in E),\n\\]\nwith \\(M_E\\) independent of \\(j\\). Since \\(k_j\\in K\\) and \\(d_U\\) is proper by Proposition 2, this is the claimed compactness.</p><p>Montel’s theorem in the bounded ambient affine coordinates, followed by diagonal extraction, now gives a holomorphic map\n\\[\ng:D_0\\longrightarrow U\n\\]\nwith \\(g_j\\to g\\) locally uniformly. The compact containment just proved puts the limit in \\(U\\), rather than merely in its closure. It also allows the composition identities to pass to the limit: on each compact subset of \\(D_0\\), the points \\(g_j(x)\\) lie in a common compact subset of \\(U\\), where \\(f_j\\to f\\). Thus\n\\[\nf\\circ g=\\operatorname{id}_{D_0}.\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nIn particular, \\(f\\) has full rank at every point of \\(g(D_0)\\).</p><p>The maps \\(f_j\\) have nonzero Jacobian wherever they are defined. Their local Jacobian determinants converge to that of \\(f\\). The zero-free limit principle says that a limit of zero-free holomorphic functions is either zero-free or identically zero; in several variables this follows by restriction to complex lines. Thus the full-rank locus and its complement are both open: a zero of a local limiting determinant forces that determinant to vanish throughout its coordinate ball. Equation (5.7) makes the full-rank locus nonempty, so connectedness of \\(U\\) implies that \\(f\\) has nonzero Jacobian everywhere.</p><p>Fix \\(q\\in U\\). Local inverse stability gives a neighborhood \\(B\\) of \\(f(q)\\) such that\n\\[\nB\\subset f_j(V_q)\\subset D_{t_j}\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nfor all sufficiently large \\(j\\), where \\(V_q\\Subset U\\) is a fixed coordinate neighborhood of \\(q\\).</p><p>Explicitly, center source coordinates at \\(q\\) and normalize the target by the inverse of \\(df_q\\). On a sufficiently small closed source ball, the normalized derivative of \\(f\\) differs from the identity by less than \\(1/4\\). Local uniform convergence of the holomorphic maps gives the same bound with \\(1/2\\) for \\(f_j\\) on that ball. For every target point in a fixed smaller ball about \\(f(q)\\), the equation \\(f_j(x)=y\\) is then solved by a contraction of the closed source ball into itself, since \\(f_j(q)\\to f(q)\\). This proves (5.8).</p><p>If \\(R(f(q))\\in F\\), the excluded-point assertion of Proposition 6 supplies points outside \\(D_{t_j}\\) converging to \\(f(q)\\), contradicting (5.8). Thus\n\\[\nf(U)\\subset\\mathcal O.\n\\]\nThe set \\(f(U)\\) is connected and contains \\(D_0\\) by (5.7), so it lies in that same connected component:\n\\[\nf(U)=D_0.\n\\]\nFinally, for each \\(q\\in U\\), the points \\(f_j(q)\\) eventually lie in a compact neighborhood of \\(f(q)\\) inside \\(D_0\\). Passing to the limit in\n\\[\ng_j(f_j(q))=q\n\\]\nyields \\(g(f(q))=q\\). Therefore \\(f:U\\to D_0\\) and \\(g:D_0\\to U\\) are inverse biholomorphisms.</p><p>The real quadratic vector \\(P_2\\) has a unique decomposition\n\\[\nP_2(z)=H(z,z)+\\operatorname{Re}B(z),\n\\]\nwhere \\(H\\) is a vector of Hermitian forms and \\(B\\) a vector of holomorphic homogeneous quadratic polynomials. The triangular holomorphic change\n\\[\n(Z,W)\\longmapsto(z,w)=(Z,W-iB(Z))\n\\]\nis globally invertible and transforms \\(D_0\\) into the domain \\(D\\) of (5.3).</p><p>The functionals in (5.6) retain their independence. We claim that throughout \\(D\\),\n\\[\n\\ell_a(\\operatorname{Im}w)\\geq c\\|z\\|^2,\n\\qquad 1\\leq a\\leq k,\n\\tag{5.9}\n\\]\nfor a common \\(c&gt;0\\).</p><p>Fix \\((z,w)\\in D\\), and consider the unscaled point\n\\[\nq_t=\\Phi\\bigl(\\sqrt t\\,z,\\ t(w+iB(z))\\bigr).\n\\]\nIt lies in \\(U\\) for every sufficiently small \\(t\\), by Proposition 6 applied before the triangular change.</p><p>The local holomorphic logarithm of a support has expansion\n\\[\n\\log h_a(\\Phi(z,w))\n=\ni\\ell_a(w)+A_a(z)\n+\nO\\bigl(\\|z\\|^3+\\|z\\|\\|w\\|+\\|w\\|^2\\bigr),\n\\]\nwhere \\(A_a\\) is holomorphic homogeneous quadratic. Dividing\n\\[\n-\\log|h_a(q_t)|\\geq\\|q_t-p\\|^2\n\\]\nby \\(t\\) and passing to the limit gives\n\\[\n\\ell_a(\\operatorname{Im}w)\n+\n\\operatorname{Re}\\bigl(\\ell_a(B(z))-A_a(z)\\bigr)\n\\geq\\|Jz\\|^2,\n\\qquad\nJ=d_z\\Phi_0.\n\\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nThe linear map \\(J\\) is injective. Thus, when \\(m&gt;0\\), its right-hand side is bounded below by \\(c\\|z\\|^2\\) with \\(c&gt;0\\). When \\(m=0\\), the same inequality holds with any positive \\(c\\), since \\(z=0\\).</p><p>For each real \\(\\theta\\), the point \\((e^{i\\theta}z,w)\\) also lies in \\(D\\), because\n\\[\nH(e^{i\\theta}z,e^{i\\theta}z)=H(z,z).\n\\]\nAverage (5.10) over \\(\\theta\\). The holomorphic quadratic terms have average zero, whereas\n\\[\n\\|J(e^{i\\theta}z)\\|=\\|Jz\\|.\n\\]\nThis proves (5.9).</p><p>Putting \\(z=0\\) gives \\(\\ell_a(y)\\geq0\\) on \\(C\\). Since \\(C\\) is open and \\(\\ell_a\\neq0\\), equality at a point of \\(C\\) would produce a nearby negative value. Hence\n\\[\n\\ell_a(y)&gt;0\\qquad(y\\in C).\n\\]\nNext fix \\(y\\in C\\). For every \\(r&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n(z,i(H(z,z)+ry))\\in D.\n\\]\nApply (5.9) and let \\(r\\downarrow0\\) to obtain\n\\[\n\\ell_a(H(z,z))\\geq c\\|z\\|^2.\n\\]\nThis proves both parts of (5.4).</p><p>Conjugating the original group by the resulting biholomorphism transfers proper discontinuity and cocompactness to \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "\\(D\\) has a bounded realization as a domain in \\(\\mathbb C^{m+k}\\) and is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.", "statement": "<p>**Symmetry of the quadratic model; the final application in original Section 6.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(m\\geq0\\) and \\(k\\geq1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C\\subset\\mathbb R^k\\) be a nonempty connected open cone satisfying \\(rC=C\\) for every \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H\\) be a vector of Hermitian forms on \\(\\mathbb C^m\\), complex linear in its first argument.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n\\[\nD=\n\\{(z,w)\\in\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k:\n\\operatorname{Im}w-H(z,z)\\in C\\}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose there are independent real linear functionals \\(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_k\\) on \\(\\mathbb R^k\\) and \\(c&gt;0\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\ell_j(y)&gt;0\\quad(y\\in C),\n\\qquad\n\\ell_j(H(z,z))\\geq c\\|z\\|^2\n\\quad(z\\in\\mathbb C^m).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">A domain is called divisible if a group of holomorphic automorphisms acts properly when given the discrete topology and has a compact covering set. This does not require a free action.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(D\\) is divisible.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(D\\) has a bounded realization as a domain in \\(\\mathbb C^{m+k}\\) and is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.</p>", "proof": "<p>First give an explicit bounded realization in the intrinsic dimension \\(m+k\\). Extend the real forms \\(\\ell_1,\\ldots,\\ell_k\\) complex linearly to \\(\\mathbb C^k\\), and write\n\\[\nu_j=\\ell_j(w).\n\\]\nFor \\((z,w)\\in D\\), the support inequalities give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}u_j\n=\n\\ell_j(\\operatorname{Im}w-H(z,z))\n+\n\\ell_j(H(z,z))\n&gt;\nc\\|z\\|^2\n\\geq0.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\n\\mathcal B(z,w)\n=\n\\left(\n\\frac{u_1-i}{u_1+i},\n\\ldots,\n\\frac{u_k-i}{u_k+i},\n\\frac{z}{u_1+i}\n\\right)\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nis holomorphic. Its first \\(k\\) coordinates have modulus less than one.</p><p>If \\(m&gt;0\\) and \\(y=\\operatorname{Im}u_1\\), then\n\\[\n\\left\\|\\frac{z}{u_1+i}\\right\\|\n\\leq\n\\frac{\\sqrt{y/c}}{1+y}\n\\leq\n\\frac1{2\\sqrt c}.\n\\]\nHere \\(|u_1+i|\\geq1+y\\), and \\(2\\sqrt y\\leq1+y\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal B(D)\\) is bounded.</p><p>Inverting the Cayley coordinates recovers each \\(u_j\\) holomorphically. Independence of the \\(\\ell_j\\) then recovers \\(w\\), and multiplication by \\(u_1+i\\) recovers \\(z\\). These formulas give a holomorphic local inverse on an open neighborhood of every image point, so \\(\\mathcal B\\) is a biholomorphism onto a bounded domain in \\(\\mathbb C^{k+m}\\). When \\(m=0\\), omit the final coordinate block and its estimate.</p><p>Choose \\(a_0\\in C\\). Then \\((0,ia_0)\\in D\\). Real translations of \\(w\\) preserve the defining residual, as do scalar unit rotations of \\(z\\), because \\(H\\) is Hermitian. Under a positive weighted dilation,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Im}(rw)\n-\nH(r^{1/2}z,r^{1/2}z)\n=\nr\\bigl(\\operatorname{Im}w-H(z,z)\\bigr).\n\\]\nSince \\(rC=C\\) for every \\(r&gt;0\\), these maps preserve \\(D\\) in both directions.</p><p>The bounded realization, the point with first coordinate zero, the three invariances, and divisibility verify every hypothesis of Lemma 8.1. Therefore \\(D\\) is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "\\(D\\) is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain.", "statement": "<p>**Vey’s theorem; original Theorem 6.1, quoted from [Vey70].**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">For this lemma, the quadratic description and support inequalities of Proposition 8 are not assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(D\\subset\\mathbb C^m\\times\\mathbb C^k\\) be a connected domain biholomorphic to a bounded domain.</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(D\\) contains a point \\((0,w)\\) and is invariant under\n\\[\n(z,w)\\longmapsto(z,w+a),\\qquad\n(z,w)\\longmapsto(e^{i\\theta}z,w),\\qquad\n(z,w)\\longmapsto(r^{1/2}z,rw)\n\\]\nfor every \\(a\\in\\mathbb R^k\\), \\(\\theta\\in\\mathbb R\\), and \\(r&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume that \\(D\\) is divisible: a group of holomorphic automorphisms acts properly with the discrete topology and has a compact covering set.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(D\\) is biholomorphic to a bounded symmetric domain. The case \\(m=0\\) is included.</p>", "proof": "<p>This is the form of Vey’s theorem used in the paper. In Vey’s terminology, the hypotheses define a divisible \\(S\\)-domain of exponent \\(1/2\\), after reversing the variable blocks.</p><p>Vey’s Theorem 1 asserts homogeneity, and the immediately following consequence asserts symmetry [Vey70, p. 479]. The zero-block convention is explicit in [Vey70, pp. 480–481]. Neither convexity of a defining cone nor regularity of its boundary is an input to this theorem.</p><p>Vey obtains the symmetry consequence from earlier work of Hano, Koszul, and Borel [Vey70, p. 479]; the paper uses his combined statement rather than separately invoking those antecedent results. Divisibility here does not require a free action.</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [1, 12, "cite"], [2, 1, "cite"], [3, 9, "cite"], [3, 10, "cite"], [3, 12, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [4, 3, "cite"], [5, 8, "cite"], [5, 9, "cite"], [5, 11, "cite"], [5, 12, "cite"], [6, 5, "cite"], [7, 5, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [8, 11, "cite"], [8, 12, "cite"], [9, 11, "cite"], [9, 12, "cite"], [10, 9, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 0, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [14, 13, "cite"]]});
